      "text": "1. For the quadratic equation $x^2 - 63x + k = 0$ to have both roots as prime numbers, their negative counterparts $-x_1$ and $-x_2$ must also be prime numbers. This means $x_1 = 63 + x_2$ and $x_2 = 63 + x_1$ must be pairs of prime numbers that add up to 126.\n\n2. We can list all the pairs of prime numbers that add up to 126: $(2, 124), (3, 123), (5, 121), (7, 119), (11, 115), (13, 113), (17, 109), (19, 107), (23, 103), (29, 97), (31, 95), (37, 89), (41, 85), (43, 83), (47, 79), (53, 73), (59, 67), (61, 65), (67, 59), (71, 55), (73, 53), (79, 57), (83, 43), (89, 37), (97, 31)$.\n\n\nNow the question is what are the possible values of $k$ where $k = (x_1)(x_2)$? Given the pairs and that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are prime numbers, $k$ should always be a prime number such that $(2)(2)=4, (3)(3)=9, (5)(5)=25, (7)(7)=49$ etc., but then $2$ is not one of the prime addends! Since the number of prime addends in the second factor multiply by the prime addends in the first factor always yields only a single prime number from the product, but not so here. This means that unless one of the addends is a prime square root, all combinations of prime factors produce duplicate values for $k$.\n   We list all the prime factors of 2025:$\\{3, 5\\}$\n\nPossible $k$ values for each are $ 15$ and $75 $.\n\n$\\boxed{2}$",
